   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. Intended use and limits\n\n*Who uses this, for what — and where it stops being valid.*\n\n**Intended users:** FlyRank editors and content reviewers who need to prioritize which pages to refresh or rewrite first.\n\n**Primary use case:** Generate a ranked queue of content rows that are most likely to be in decline (based on the `is_declining_label` target), using a combination of random forest model probability and a non-leaky rule-based baseline score. The queue is a decision-support aid — not an automatic publishing trigger.\n\n**Valid decision contexts:**\n- Pages with `suggested_action` = `refresh` or `refresh_and_review_ctr` / `refresh_and_review_engagement` should be prioritized for content audit.\n- Pages with `suggested_action` = `monitor` or `expand_and_refresh` are lower priority but still useful for periodic review.\n\n**Where it stops being valid:**\n- **Never use model score as an automatic publishing decision.** The model predicts decline risk, it does not validate content quality or compliance.\n- **No client-identifying details** appear in the queue — content_id values are anonymized hashes.\n- **No URL, title, or keyword claims.** The model works on aggregated feature vectors only.\n- **Cross-sectional snapshot only.** The `is_declining` label is a same-30-day-window proxy (trend_direction == \"down\" from 30-day vs previous 30-day impression change). It does not predict future trajectory after a refresh.\n- **Survivor bias.** Pages that remain in the snapshot at 365+ days are survivors — the model may not represent all old pages equally.\n- **Label-window overlap.** 11/18 numeric features aggregate over 30/90-day windows that overlap the label formation window. Numbers are directional/decision-support, not causal.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. Human review + the no-go list\n\n*What a person must check before acting. What should never be automated.*\n\n**Human must verify before acting on a recommendation:**\n1. **Manual content audit** — open the page and check whether the suggested action (refresh, expand, monitor) is appropriate.\n2. **Editorial context** — does this page align with current brand strategy, business priorities, and seasonal campaigns?\n3. **Traffic source mix** — verify that the page's traffic profile matches the model's expectations (e.g., avoid applying `refresh_and_review_ctr` to pages whose primary traffic is referral/social, not search).\n4. **Date of last refresh** — if a page has been refreshed in the last 90 days, deprioritize even if the model scores it high.\n5. **Client or business constraints** — some pages may be on a fixed content calendar and cannot be touched during certain windows.\n\n**No-go list — never automate based solely on this model's output:**\n- Do NOT publish or unpage a URL based solely on `suggested_action` or `final_refresh_score` > 80.\n- Do NOT claim that refreshing a page will \"recover traffic\" or \"improve Google ranking.\" The model supports editorial prioritization, not algorithm causality.\n- Do NOT feed `content_id`, `client_id`, or any pseudonymous IDs into downstream systems as real identifiers.\n- Do NOT reduce the confidence thresholds below 0.5 for high-stakes publishing decisions without additional validation.\n- Do NOT ignore the leakage warnings: 11/18 features overlap the 30-day label window. Report all metrics with base rate context.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 4. Monitoring / retrain triggers\n\n*What would tell you the recommendations went stale?*\n\n**Monitor these indicators to know when the queue needs retraining:**\n- **Cross-client P@50 drift:** If the new cross-client P@50 (GroupKFold) drops more than 0.05 from the baseline (0.58), retrain with fresh data.\n- **Label rate shift:** If the declining-label rate moves more than ±5 percentage points from the baseline (0.54), investigate data changes before retraining.\n- **New feature availability** — if a new search signal becomes available (e.g., AI referral traffic metrics), run a leakage audit before adding it to the feature set.\n- **Baseline score divergence:** If the baseline refresh score distribution shifts significantly (e.g., median moves >0.1), re-examine the percentile_rank normalization against the current data range.\n- **Seasonal patterns** — run the same pipeline quarterly; compare action mixes across runs. If the top 3 actions change substantially without data schema changes, investigate.\n\n**Retrain trigger:** Any two of the above indicators shifting simultaneously should prompt a full pipeline re-run (scripts/01 through 04).\n\n**Stale-warning sign:** If the top-10 queue is dominated by the same `suggested_action` = `monitor` rows with no `refresh` or `refresh_and_review_*` actions, the model may have converged to a local optimum — consider feature engineering refresh or label definition review.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
